WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.240
Hey, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents, episode 69 for June 3, 2026. Your daily AI briefing.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.260
The AI world never sleeps. Here's what you need to know today.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.560
Microsoft is shipping hardware built from the silicon up to run AI Agents instead of conventional apps.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.640
The company unveiled Project Solara as a full chip-to-cloud platform for agent first enterprise devices.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.820
Open AI added three new codex plugins aimed at investing, sales, and creative production tasks.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.340
Uber set a $1,500 monthly cap per employee on each coding agent tool

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.100
and Thropic Broaden Project Glasswing Access while responding to the new White House AI executive order.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.100
Microsoft and Mayo Clinic are jointly building a frontier healthcare model.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.760
Project Solara spans custom silicon through cloud orchestration.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.680
The system targets workloads where agents replace conventional software interfaces entirely.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.220
This marks a concrete step beyond software-only agent frameworks.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.340
It focuses on persistent agent execution, memory management, and tool orchestration.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.720
Builders working on internal enterprise automation should watch for early access programs or reference

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.620
designs. The move signals Microsoft's intent to compete directly in the agent hardware layer

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.360
alongside its software efforts. Microsoft and Mayo Clinic announced a partnership to build a

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:34.780
frontier AI model for healthcare. The effort combines Microsoft's scaling infrastructure with Mayo's

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.580
clinical data and domain expertise. The focus is medical reasoning and workflow tasks.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.840
No parameter count, benchmarks, or release timeline were disclosed.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.460
Healthcare teams should monitor for early evaluation access once internal testing begins.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:57.360
Anthropic extended clawed mythos preview access to roughly 150 additional organizations.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:04.080
The organizations span more than 15 countries under the expanded project Glasswing program.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.040
The move broadens testing of the preview model beyond the initial cohort.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.800
Teams working on high stakes or regulated domains should track future updates for potential early access.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.560
Researchers released typewriter LM, a 7.24 billion parameter model.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.320
It was trained exclusively on English text predating 1913.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:34.300
The release includes a 54 billion token typewriter corpus and supporting evaluation resources.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.720
The work covers leakage mitigation, temporally consistent post-training,

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.860
and a new history event benchmark.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.520
The model and data sets are publicly available for research on historical language modeling.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:53.080
Uber instituted a $1,500 monthly spending cap per employee per AI coding tool.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.840
The cap applies only to agent coding software such as cursor and clawed code.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.240
The policy responds to rapid budget overruns that exceeded 2025 forecasts.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.740
Engineering teams evaluating agent adoption should model similar per-tool budgets.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:14.300
OpenAI released a public equity investing plugin for codex.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.040
The plugin moves users from natural language questions directly to model-generated investment

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.040
analysis. It joins the growing set of domain-specific codex extensions.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.780
Finance and investment workflow builders can test the plugin through the codex interface.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.320
OpenAI launched a sales plugin for codex.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.560
The tool helps users prepare faster and sell smarter through agent-assisted workflows.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.100
The focus is sales-specific tasks within the codex environment.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.900
Sales operations teams should experiment with the plugin on representative deal prep tasks.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.180
A new post outlines rules for keeping agents effective by defining clear boundaries.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.900
The guidance emphasizes preventing agents from executing unverified external actions.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.420
It also warns against making irreversible decisions without oversight.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.120
Practitioners should review their current agent permission models against the recommended guardrails.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.620
SEA embedding provides a fully open and reproducible text embedding pipeline.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.780
It was trained only on public data for Southeast Asian languages.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.620
The work studies data composition, training objectives, and base encoder choices.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:31.440
It achieves state-of-the-art results on the SEA BED benchmark.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:37.800
Researchers working with SEA languages can reproduce the pipeline without proprietary data.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:44.060
A community benchmark tested LLMs on strict two-dimensional spatial constraint adherence.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:50.600
It used a custom sulcobon puzzle that forbids chain of thought and requires exact directional output.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:58.540
Top-close models including chat GPT, Shwen 3.7 max, and Gemini 3.5 thinking succeeded.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.600
Several other variants failed on illegal moves or formatting.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.940
The prompt and map are shared for others to replicate.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:10.320
Okay, let's pop the hood on hallucination mitigation for a moment.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.260
One recent approach treats hallucinations as orthogonal noise vectors in the residual stream.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.480
These vectors can be identified and suppressed at inference time.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.880
The method uses the current input residual as a dynamic anchor.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.360
It then performs layer-wise orthogonal decomposition on attention-head outputs.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:37.520
A Z-score threshold attenuates only the outlier components that point away from the context subspace.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:42.440
This adds a modest per layer computation cost but avoids retraining.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:47.340
It preserves performance on knowledge-heavy tasks where blanket suppression would hurt.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.920
The technique shows the largest gains on summarization and instruction following benchmarks.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:56.840
It maintains trivia QA and truthful QA scores.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.240
Teams already running inference-time interventions should test whether adding a lightweight

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.400
or thogonal projection step improves faithfulness.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:11.120
The main gotcha is that effectiveness depends on having a reasonably accurate running estimate

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.520
of the context subspace.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.880
Test the new codex sales and creative plugins on a real workflow.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:22.120
Run the shared soak-a-bond prompt against any new models you have access to.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:28.180
Review your current coding agent spend against Uber's $1,500 per tool monthly cap.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.760
Experiment with SEHA embedding on any Southeast Asian language retrieval tasks.

